introduction:
Foundation models such as Vision-Language-Action Models (VLAs) [1]
show incredible promise for robot control but require vast amounts of robot data, which is not easily obtainable. Teleoperation is a simple strategy for collecting robot data in the form of demonstrations but incurs a large human cost per task,
making it difficult to scale. To reduce the amount of human effort, recent work developed semi-automated demonstration generation systems that adapt a handful of human-teleoperated demonstrations to new initial states [2].
However, these approaches still require annotated human demonstrations per task, preventing them from scaling autonomously to new tasks, and are typically deployed only in simulation because they produce unsafe behaviors.

Task and Motion Planning (TAMP) [3] systems are able to autonomously solve new tasks using the same planning model.
Prior work has used TAMP to generate demonstrations for imitation learning [4, 5], but due to the stochastic nature of prior TAMP algorithms, the resulting demonstrations have been difficult to learn from due to their suboptimality and multi-modality.
Recent work on TAMP leverages hardware acceleration, for example, on Graphics Processing Units (GPUs), to more efficiently solve problems [6, 7, 8].
We directly investigate the impact of GPU acceleration not only on producing demonstrations quickly but also on optimizing them so that they are easier to learn from.

Our approach, DemoStream, first makes novel TAMP contributions in the form of batched streams, conditional samplers that generate constraint-satisfying outputs for a batch of input values, and batched computation graph optimization algorithms that compose multiple batched streams and cost functions in order to explore thousands of TAMP solutions in parallel.
These representation and algorithmic innovations give us the flexibility to explore multiple TAMP cost functions and analyze their effect on demonstration success rate, demonstration length, and downstream policy success rate.
Notably, we find that without solution optimization, TAMP systems still have high demonstration generation success rates but train policies with success rates of zero (Table I).
Additionally, the choice of cost function matters; we find that it is easier to learn from demonstrations that minimize task-space rather than joint-space costs, both during TAMP and motion planning, likely because these costs minimize the motion of wrist-mounted cameras, shrinking the distribution of encountered observations.
DemoStream is able to train successful visuomotor policies on a variety of tasks including multi-robot (Figure 5), long-horizon (Figure 6), agentic open-world (Figure 7), and real-world (Figure 1) tasks.

The contributions of this paper are:

New TAMP batched samplers and computation graph optimizers for GPU-accelerated plan optimization;

Automated demonstration generation via agentic, batched TAMP for end-to-end imitation learning;

Analysis on the effect of action space, cost function, and batch size on policy learning success rates;

Simulation experiments demonstrating long-horizon, multi-robot, and open-world DemoStream capabilities;

Safe automated real-world demonstration collection.

1. New TAMP batched samplers and computation graph optimizers for GPU-accelerated plan optimization;

2. Automated demonstration generation via agentic, batched TAMP for end-to-end imitation learning;

3. Analysis on the effect of action space, cost function, and batch size on policy learning success rates;

4. Simulation experiments demonstrating long-horizon, multi-robot, and open-world DemoStream capabilities;

5. Safe automated real-world demonstration collection.

The collection of spatiotemporally varying data is critical for environmental and engineering applications, from characterizing ocean currents for climate modeling [1, 2] to mapping wind speeds for offshore wind farm siting [3, 4]. Because these tasks require spatially expansive and temporally persistent measurements, sustainably powered mobile robots—such as solar-powered autonomous surface vessels (ASVs) [5] and wind-driven sailing drones [6]—have become increasingly attractive.

Unlike traditional finite-horizon missions, persistently powered robots must continuously balance energy inflow (harvesting) with energy outflow (propulsion and sensing). Similarly, a central premise behind persistent missions is the presence of information gain resulting from observations and information decay resulting from locations going unvisited over time. We view this interaction through a coupled “reservoir” lens (Fig. 1). Harvested energy fills a physical battery reservoir, and this energy is converted into information, which possesses its own virtual reservoir. Because environmental information continuously decays due to forecast drift or measurement staleness [7], long-term monitoring requires the robot to repeatedly revisit and refresh informative regions.

While a large body of literature addresses informative path planning [8, 9] and energy-aware routing [10, 11], these frameworks often model missions as resource-constrained traveling salesperson problems [12]. Such routing formulations treat sensing as a discrete event collected at isolated nodes. Conversely, continuous approaches like ergodic search optimize trajectories over time-varying information maps [13], but typically rely on finite-horizon optimization formulations. However, for a mobile sensor accruing continuous rewards, a discrete objective is insufficient, since a measurement taken at any point along a path affects not only the evolution of information at that point on the path but also the evolution of information at every other location.

To explicitly capture these dynamics, we leverage the quantity of clarity [14], which describes the state of certainty about a particular point at a particular time and evolves according to an ODE containing both information gain and decay terms. Coupled with a standard nonlinear propulsion model (e.g., drag power proportional to speed cubed [15]), the fundamental control problem shifts from discrete routing to computing a continuous speed trajectory that maximizes a reward functional that characterizes information over the entire path at each point in time.

Specifically, we consider a mobile robot that is tasked with optimizing its speed profile over a prescribed path, given limited available power, (generally) unequal importance allocated to certain portions of the path relative to others, and periodicity constraints on final vs. initial energy (robot state of charge) and clarity (information) states. The theoretical results center around the derivation and interpretation of necessary conditions for optimality under this problem formulation. After deriving these conditions, we present several properties associated with the nature of the optimal control strategy, effect of unequal importance weighting along the mission path, and limiting performance with respect to both the importance weighting and available power.

The theoretical results in this work are complemented by two simulation studies. The first study represents an illustrative “toy” example that shows how the robot’s speed profile varies according to the importance of information at different points along the path. The second study focuses on using a solar-powered autonomous surface vessel (ASV) to characterize salinity information in the Chesapeake Bay. Results of the simulation study illustrate the value of modifying the ASV’s speed profile by solving the optimization problem presented in this work.

In summary, the contributions of this paper are as follows:

A modeling framework coupling clarity dynamics with nonlinear energy expenditure;

An optimal control formulation for periodic sensing missions derived via Pontryagin’s Maximum Principle, yielding necessary conditions for optimality;

Analytical results establishing several properties of the optimal control solution;

Numerical validation via IPOPT, demonstrating optimal speed profiles that slow in high-information regions and accelerate through low-value regions;

A simulation case study for salinity monitoring in the Chesapeake Bay, showcasing the utility of the work in a realistic application domain.
